WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.155
- Hey everybody it's Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:00:01.155 --> 00:00:04.440
and this is one of my favorite
formats of the content I do.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.840
This is Pod Sessions, episode number 10.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:08.673
This one's fun.

00:00:08.673 --> 00:00:11.541
On a dinner that was done in, was that DC?

00:00:11.541 --> 00:00:12.374
- August, mm-hmm.
- Yep.

00:00:12.374 --> 00:00:13.910
- In DC in August, two of the

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:15.290
three wonderful individuals were here.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:16.570
We were kind of shooting the shit.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:18.070
I was getting to know one

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:19.559
a little bit better, one completely.

00:00:19.559 --> 00:00:21.670
It was a format of a conference series

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.310
I was doing around the
country where I was speaking,

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.180
and then there was a
dinner series at night.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.510
And they were there.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:29.140
We were jamming, and it just
kind of like popped in my head.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.490
And we were talking about
our third individual,

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:31.670
and I said, you know what?

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:34.450
Why don't we just all get
together and do a Pod Sessions

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:36.900
because I remember thinking wow.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.204
A lot of this conversation,
a lot of what I thought

00:00:39.204 --> 00:00:41.690
the third individual could
bring to the conversation

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:43.280
would make tremendous
content for the things

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.800
that I care about whether it's marketing

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.860
or actually building an actual
business or practicality

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.472
or siphoning from a
career to an entrepreneur.

00:00:50.472 --> 00:00:53.074
Just a lot of themes, so I'm
super excited about this.

00:00:53.074 --> 00:00:55.047
I've got a good sense of
where I wanna take it.

00:00:55.047 --> 00:00:58.644
This one's a little more
under the radar Pod Sessions

00:00:58.644 --> 00:01:01.360
meaning we've been bringing people

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.810
with huge audience and
things of that nature.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:05.950
This is a little bit more of individuals

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:08.580
that I admire pieces of,
and I thought if we put it

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:11.240
all together we could
really make a nice meal.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:12.210
So I'm excited.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.430
Why don't we go around
and introduce ourselves?

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.470
And why don't we start to the left

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.950
and go into a kind of circle mode here.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.196
So how are you young man?
- Sounds good.

00:01:21.196 --> 00:01:23.440
- [Gary] Tell the Vayner
nation who you are.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.550
- So, my name's Dylan Leazier.

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:28.480
I'm from Hagerstown Maryland,
small town in Maryland.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.268
Most of you probably
never heard of it before.

00:01:30.268 --> 00:01:33.260
Just recently moved to New York and--

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:34.093
- They've heard of that.

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:35.870
- Yeah, they have. (laughs)

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:39.117
And working for one of your
good friends Eytan Sugarman,

00:01:39.117 --> 00:01:41.810
managing socials for
the Hunt and Fish Club

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:43.810
and for Southern Hospitality.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.580
- And that's because
your background is also

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:47.900
in a little bit of that world right?

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.155
- Yeah, so for like the past two years

00:01:50.155 --> 00:01:52.107
up until I moved to New York,

00:01:52.107 --> 00:01:54.450
I was rebranding and
renovating my father's

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.140
restaurant and bar back home in Maryland

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:00.790
and ended up at that dinner with you.

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:04.790
And then things transpired
and took a leap of faith

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:07.490
and was like I'll move to
New York and take this step.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:08.323
- Love it.

00:02:08.323 --> 00:02:09.280
Let's keep going.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:12.886
- Everybody, Rabiah Sutton,
what else am I saying?

00:02:12.886 --> 00:02:14.922
I know. I forgot that fast.

00:02:14.922 --> 00:02:16.875
I'm originally from DC.

00:02:16.875 --> 00:02:19.690
Moved to Northern Virginia when I was ten.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:23.858
Own a business called Forward
Think selling technology

00:02:23.858 --> 00:02:27.400
services and products to
the government primarily.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.170
- How long you been doing that?

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.450
- I've had my own company for eight years.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.302
But I've been in the field for over 20.

00:02:34.302 --> 00:02:35.304
- [Gary] Yep.

00:02:35.304 --> 00:02:36.137
- Yep.

00:02:36.137 --> 00:02:36.970
- [Gary] And how are you?

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:38.403
- I'm great, happy to be here.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:40.423
- Sir?

00:02:40.423 --> 00:02:42.959
- Steve Sheetz, from
Altoona, Pennsylvania.

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:46.599
Chairman now of the Sheetz Family Council.

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:49.210
I've been like bottle boy and dishwasher

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.920
and helped build this
business from one store

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.623
to 586 stores through the years.

00:02:54.623 --> 00:02:56.470
- And for the people that
live in different parts

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:58.460
of the world, what are the stores?

00:02:58.460 --> 00:02:59.850
- Good question, yes.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:01.870
Like you, you've probably never seen one.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.280
- [Gary] I haven't.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.550
- So it's a convenience store.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:08.180
We started out as a dairy store.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:10.473
And then as times changed and people's

00:03:10.473 --> 00:03:13.770
- Needs.
- demands for food changed,

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:18.060
- then we had to add gasoline in the 70s

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.530
when the blue laws went away.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.320
We loved the blue laws,
Sheetz, because everybody

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.200
was closed Sundays except us.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:25.203
- [Gary] Interesting.

00:03:25.203 --> 00:03:26.036
- So everybody came to Sheetz.

00:03:26.036 --> 00:03:29.140
So it was a dairy store with a big deli,

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.830
and then now we've really--

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:32.730
- And why were you able to, the blue laws,

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.638
'cause I know 'em because
of the liquor industry.

00:03:34.638 --> 00:03:38.120
Why was Sheetz able to be open?

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.440
Because of the gasoline?

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:42.080
- Well because we had
less than 15 employees

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.070
at the time, per store.

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:44.910
In other words--

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:47.120
- Was that the essence of the blue laws?

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:48.094
- Yeah.

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:48.927
The essence of the blue laws

00:03:48.927 --> 00:03:50.268
were different in different states, but--

00:03:50.268 --> 00:03:51.269
- [Gary] Yeah, that's why--

00:03:51.269 --> 00:03:54.590
- I think in Pennsylvania it
was you had to have like less

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:56.630
than ten or 15 employees, so there were

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:58.540
no supermarkets, no Walmarts.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:03:59.373
It was all us.

00:03:59.373 --> 00:04:02.641
So a Sunday was like
five, ten times normal day

00:04:02.641 --> 00:04:04.160
because we're the only thing open.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:05.030
We loved it.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:06.660
And then the blue laws.
- And did you have

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:07.493
liquor license, too?

00:04:07.493 --> 00:04:08.757
- We don't.
- Oh, yeah, that's right.

00:04:08.757 --> 00:04:10.010
Pennsylvania state don't
controlled the purchase, right.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:12.130
- Well right now, we just hit
a hundred stores with beer.

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:13.390
We've been fighting it since.

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:15.410
I came out of college in 1969.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:18.533
We had three stores,
and we pushed for beer,

00:04:18.533 --> 00:04:20.320
and we pushed for self serve gas.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:21.153
We got self serve gas.
- Wait a minute.

00:04:21.153 --> 00:04:22.640
This is a very important
part of the story.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:23.962
- [Steve] Yep.

00:04:23.962 --> 00:04:25.870
- You graduated college, you
said, or was it high school?

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:27.149
- Penn State, 1969.

00:04:27.149 --> 00:04:30.890
- Right, so you graduated college the year

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:32.566
the New York Jets won the Super Bowl.

00:04:32.566 --> 00:04:33.430
- [Steve] There you go.

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:36.000
- January 12th that
year, the New York Jets

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.280
changed the football landscape forever.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:39.113
I love that.

00:04:39.113 --> 00:04:39.946
- Joe Namath.

00:04:39.946 --> 00:04:41.357
- Joe Namath.

00:04:41.357 --> 00:04:43.570
Some of my favorite Jets of
all time are Penn State alums.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:46.340
Lance Mehl, one of my favorite favorites.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:50.090
Anyway none the less, so, got it.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:53.900
And so your parents started it?

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.450
- No, yeah, it's a long story,

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.399
but we came out of the dairy business.

00:04:58.399 --> 00:05:01.150
My grandfather back in 1907.

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.670
My father then saw the
whole business changing.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.940
He opened one store and then five

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:07.710
stores during World War II.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:08.880
Then my brother came along.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.050
Took over one.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:12.440
So 1960, I went to work with him.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:13.560
We had one store.
- With your brother?

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:14.393
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:05:14.393 --> 00:05:15.520
- And then he said, I went to school

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.140
to actually to be a math teacher.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:18.180
- [Gary] You did?

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:19.190
- He said, come with me.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:21.100
We're gonna open ten stores.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.280
And so I went with him.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:23.250
- [Gary] That's awesome.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.500
- We opened ten and he
said, let's open a hundred.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.676
And we opened a hundred and he said,

00:05:27.676 --> 00:05:28.835
I'm going to Florida.

00:05:28.835 --> 00:05:29.834
And he left.

00:05:29.834 --> 00:05:30.835
- Is that what happened?

00:05:30.835 --> 00:05:31.836
(laughs)

00:05:31.836 --> 00:05:32.838
- That was back in 1983,

00:05:32.838 --> 00:05:33.839
and he's still there.
- And then you jumped in?

00:05:33.839 --> 00:05:34.838
- And we still just send
him the checks down there.

00:05:34.838 --> 00:05:35.838
He's happy.
- That's right.

00:05:35.838 --> 00:05:36.671
So you jumped in in '83 to kind of--

00:05:36.671 --> 00:05:37.839
- Yeah.

00:05:37.839 --> 00:05:39.633
Well, yeah, I was really managing all,

00:05:39.633 --> 00:05:43.120
but I learned that year the
difference, the next five years,

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.380
between a manager and a leader,
which I really didn't know.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:47.533
But it was going to hit me hard.

00:05:47.533 --> 00:05:49.900
- That was a super
interesting segue, right?

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:53.740
So this is something that I
talk to a lot of kids about,

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:58.530
transitioning from a doer to a
manager and then from manager

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:02.016
to being the last line of
the defense, the leader.

00:06:02.016 --> 00:06:04.725
Here, a bunch of kids of are listening,

00:06:04.725 --> 00:06:06.340
a bunch of people are listening,

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:08.535
but I have so many
young listeners as well.

00:06:08.535 --> 00:06:11.130
I'm listening, and I'm saying,

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.661
you were in the business
every day for 14 years

00:06:13.661 --> 00:06:17.560
presumably as minimally the number two,

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.990
probably closer to the one B.

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:23.072
And even though at that
age, on that transition,

00:06:23.072 --> 00:06:27.900
going from either two or one B to one A,

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:30.110
there was a five year
learning curve in the way

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:33.020
that you put it on making
that transition from just

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:36.330
that small of a jump perceived.

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:38.570
But I know and you know is actually

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:40.310
the biggest jump of them all.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:41.520
- That's correct.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:45.040
- What was the biggest
shocking thing from being?

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:46.530
How would you consider yourself?

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:47.720
Do you consider you were a one B,

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:49.600
or do you consider you were the two?

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:50.540
Just something out of curiosity.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:52.020
I'm sure it's just semantics.
- Well, yeah.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:53.240
I would say one B.
- Cool.

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:54.530
- 'Cause I ran all of our operations.

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:56.020
- I would say so, too.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.950
So what was the biggest
shock in that transition?

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:02.140
- Well, the biggest shock was
that I thought I was a good

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:06.860
manager, actually a great
manager, which meant that I took

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:09.290
this cookie cutter that we had developed

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:10.140
and opened the store.
- System.

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:12.430
- I knew how to hire people.

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:15.130
I knew how to do the architecture even.

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:16.161
I knew how to lay it out.

00:07:16.161 --> 00:07:17.390
- I mean, you nailed it.

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:18.223
- [Steve] Everyday boom, boom.

00:07:18.223 --> 00:07:19.694
- That's right.

00:07:19.694 --> 00:07:20.527
To create that kind of
factory, to open those kind

00:07:20.527 --> 00:07:22.700
of stores, people,
locations, that's a system.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:25.040
- But the world around me was changing.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:27.247
But as a manager, I
just kept putting those

00:07:27.247 --> 00:07:29.350
cookie cutter out everyday.

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:32.830
But the world was changing,
and I wasn't changing with it.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:34.759
You know, I was managing today's business

00:07:34.759 --> 00:07:37.819
but wasn't thinking about
where we were headed

00:07:37.819 --> 00:07:38.930
tomorrow.
- That's right.

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:40.120
- And we were in that.

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:42.332
We had to find our way
to make that transition.

00:07:42.332 --> 00:07:44.390
And by the end of that five years,

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:46.674
I finally knew where
the hell we were going.

00:07:46.674 --> 00:07:48.076
- And where was that?

00:07:48.076 --> 00:07:49.130
- [Steve] We had to transition.

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:50.130
- Do you remember?

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:52.130
- Yeah, we had to go
from a take-home store

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:55.649
where people were making
sandwiches and eating at home

00:07:55.649 --> 00:07:59.260
to a store where people
were eating on the run.

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:00.930
It was a grab and go.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:05.180
But now we had 158 stores, and
we had to make a transition.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:07.110
When we did the new model--

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:08.310
- You had to go from, for kids at home.

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:11.660
You had to go from being
more like a deli mentality

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:14.220
to a 7-Eleven of today mentality.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:15.973
- Yeah, and with better
food, hopefully, yes.

00:08:15.973 --> 00:08:16.806
- [Gary] Yes, of course.

00:08:16.806 --> 00:08:17.640
- But absolutely.

00:08:18.477 --> 00:08:19.480
(laughs)

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:21.720
- But by the way, that's
exactly right 'cause you had the

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.170
advantage of coming from
a better food place.

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:25.940
- But we had the talk
with customers to find out

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:27.969
where we were supposed
to go, and employees,

00:08:27.969 --> 00:08:29.040
and then they told us.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:32.300
So now we develop this
whole new model in 1988.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:34.470
Now we had one store that fit

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:35.730
the vision of where we were headed.

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:36.563
- Which store was that?

00:08:36.563 --> 00:08:37.520
- That was--

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:38.930
- [Gary] Do you remember?

00:08:38.930 --> 00:08:40.935
- Well, it was store 158.
- It wasn't

00:08:40.935 --> 00:08:41.976
the original store.

00:08:41.976 --> 00:08:42.979
- Oh, I see what you did.

00:08:42.979 --> 00:08:44.766
You didn't renovate.
- 158 in Louis Town.

00:08:44.766 --> 00:08:45.767
- You made the next one.

00:08:45.767 --> 00:08:46.769
- The next one.

00:08:46.769 --> 00:08:47.602
It had everything the
customers told us they wanted.

00:08:47.602 --> 00:08:48.896
- And how did it do?

00:08:48.896 --> 00:08:51.860
- It did great, but now I had
157 I had to figure out what

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:53.712
the hell to do with those.
- Oh, shit.

00:08:53.712 --> 00:08:54.825
Which is hard.

00:08:54.825 --> 00:08:56.263
- And so we really slowed down.

00:08:56.263 --> 00:08:57.266
To slow down expansion, it meant we

00:08:57.266 --> 00:09:00.447
had to go back and reassess every store.

00:09:00.447 --> 00:09:01.280
- [Gary] That's right.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:02.797
- Could they fit that new vision?

00:09:02.797 --> 00:09:03.835
- Did you sell off any stores?

00:09:03.835 --> 00:09:05.030
- We did.

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:05.863
Very good question.

00:09:05.863 --> 00:09:06.696
- Listen, I'm an operator.

00:09:06.696 --> 00:09:07.912
- We had 15 in Pittsburgh that did not fit

00:09:07.912 --> 00:09:09.910
where we were headed.
- Yeah.

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:11.200
- Very difficult to manage.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.275
- We had 11 restaurants
that we had broken off into.

00:09:14.275 --> 00:09:15.108
- Wow, wow.

00:09:15.108 --> 00:09:16.631
- We sold those.
- Yeah.

00:09:16.631 --> 00:09:18.336
- And we concentrated on
- You got focused.

00:09:18.336 --> 00:09:19.169
- here is the vision.
- Impressive.

00:09:19.169 --> 00:09:20.110
- This is where we're headed.

