- Just being connected.

00:20:27.413 --> 00:20:29.638
- Spending money on dumb shit after hours.

00:20:29.638 --> 00:20:30.637
- [Dylan] Yeah, yeah, 100%.

00:20:30.637 --> 00:20:31.868
- I get it. I get it.

00:20:31.868 --> 00:20:33.950
(laughs)

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:36.310
What is the calibration of
the New York speed so far?

00:20:36.310 --> 00:20:37.600
Has it been super fast?

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:38.840
Are you addicted to it?

00:20:38.840 --> 00:20:39.811
Is it like holy shit?
- I love it.

00:20:39.811 --> 00:20:40.644
I love it.

00:20:40.644 --> 00:20:42.215
I was always on the go back home, right,

00:20:42.215 --> 00:20:45.149
and now, I'm on the go, but I'm

00:20:45.149 --> 00:20:47.196
in the city, right?
- Right.

00:20:47.196 --> 00:20:48.029
- It's not just like suburbs.

00:20:48.029 --> 00:20:49.965
- You're running at the
same speed as the thing.

00:20:49.965 --> 00:20:51.486
- [Dylan] Yeah, mmm-hmm.

00:20:51.486 --> 00:20:52.492
- Let's go back around.

00:20:52.492 --> 00:20:53.493
This is gona be a lot of fun.

00:20:53.493 --> 00:20:54.326
I'm gonna take it into a
different place real quick,

00:20:54.326 --> 00:20:55.550
and then we'll keep going.

00:20:55.550 --> 00:20:58.210
I want to know what you're
obsessed with right now.

00:20:58.210 --> 00:21:00.400
And I don't mean in the context
of everything we've just

00:21:00.400 --> 00:21:02.670
talked about, like you're
pumped about some new Excel

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:04.173
formula that's helping you scare.

00:21:04.173 --> 00:21:05.173
- [Rabiah] I'm not.

00:21:05.173 --> 00:21:06.807
- I'm sure.

00:21:06.807 --> 00:21:08.870
What are you just excited about
that's happening in culture

00:21:08.870 --> 00:21:11.790
whether it's a TV show,
whether it's a new product

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:13.440
that you use in the bathroom,

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:17.440
whether it's an experience you had?

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:21.273
What I love is the zeitgeist.

00:21:21.273 --> 00:21:24.470
What I loved, sir, listening to you talk,

00:21:24.470 --> 00:21:26.450
I go, oh, I know why he was successful.

00:21:26.450 --> 00:21:29.810
If you actually are consumer-centric,
you will always win.

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:32.290
The only reason I get to
sit here and people think,

00:21:32.290 --> 00:21:33.690
I've got one pitch.

00:21:33.690 --> 00:21:35.480
I always say I'm Mariano Rivera

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:36.900
I got one pitch.

00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:40.050
I don't care about my feelings.

00:21:40.050 --> 00:21:42.960
I'm pure humility to the end consumer.

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:44.420
I grew up with lemonade stands,

00:21:44.420 --> 00:21:46.610
baseball card shows, and a liquor store.

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:48.720
I've been retail for my whole life.

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:52.020
All I know is watching the
eyes, watching the reactions,

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:55.810
using my intuition to what
felt good to the other person.

00:21:55.810 --> 00:22:00.810
The market is my king, lord,
religion, nothing else.

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:02.704
So I always love this
question because I think

00:22:02.704 --> 00:22:06.010
that's what I'm trying
to pay attention to.

00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:07.770
Why are people wearing rips in their jeans

00:22:07.770 --> 00:22:09.282
again like they were in the 80s?

00:22:09.282 --> 00:22:11.530
Why are long hair back?

00:22:11.530 --> 00:22:15.394
Haircuts, styles, foods,
like systems, all that stuff,

00:22:15.394 --> 00:22:16.536
I love all that stuff.

00:22:16.536 --> 00:22:20.840
So that's what I love, and
when you said I had to listen

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:22.910
to the customers and my employees,
you're absolutely right.

00:22:22.910 --> 00:22:24.010
That's where all the answers are.

00:22:24.010 --> 00:22:26.498
They're not in Bain and McKinsey's
fancy decks from Harvard.

00:22:26.498 --> 00:22:28.520
They're in the trenches.

00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:29.448
They always will be.

00:22:29.448 --> 00:22:34.200
But on that same kick, the
reason I made that statement

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:35.450
was one, I wanted to pay you a compliment

00:22:35.450 --> 00:22:37.685
because I really loved you saying that.

00:22:37.685 --> 00:22:39.220
Two, it's about listening.

00:22:39.220 --> 00:22:41.210
So what are you listening to
now, or what are you seeing?

00:22:41.210 --> 00:22:42.550
What are you obsessed by?

00:22:42.550 --> 00:22:43.383
Let's start with you.

00:22:43.383 --> 00:22:45.263
What's the most
interesting thing going on?

00:22:47.440 --> 00:22:48.273
- That's tough.

00:22:48.273 --> 00:22:49.850
I think for me, it's just like,

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:53.450
not even to be selfish,
but my life right now

00:22:53.450 --> 00:22:55.170
because everything in my life has changed

00:22:55.170 --> 00:22:56.730
drastically in the past two months.

00:22:56.730 --> 00:22:57.563
- Yeah, yeah.

00:22:57.563 --> 00:23:00.040
- So just trying to get
acclimated to where I used to be

00:23:00.040 --> 00:23:02.770
and where I'm at now and just being--

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:04.110
- Are you just enjoying it tremendously?

00:23:04.110 --> 00:23:06.400
- Yeah, it's like I wake up every day.

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:08.010
I'm happy. I'm passionate.

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:08.890
- The action?

00:23:08.890 --> 00:23:10.210
- Yes, 100%.

00:23:10.210 --> 00:23:11.172
- I love it.

00:23:11.172 --> 00:23:15.444
- I am obsessed with,
besides your content, is--

00:23:15.444 --> 00:23:16.277
(laughs)

00:23:16.277 --> 00:23:17.447
- Thank you.

00:23:17.447 --> 00:23:20.740
- A form of intermittent
fasting called four, three

00:23:20.740 --> 00:23:22.770
where you eat fours days a week

00:23:22.770 --> 00:23:25.620
and fast three days a week for 36 hours.

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:28.180
- Okay so this is super
interesting because I don't know

00:23:28.180 --> 00:23:30.040
what this is, but I'm
gonna keep asking you.

00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:32.010
The reason, I apologize for jumping in,

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:33.942
is I'm actively fasting today for

00:23:33.942 --> 00:23:35.910
this is the third week in a row.

00:23:35.910 --> 00:23:38.799
I used to fast a lot, one day a week,

00:23:38.799 --> 00:23:41.370
a couple years ago when
I really lost my weight.

00:23:41.370 --> 00:23:42.570
It really worked.

00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:44.410
The last couple years, the last 18 months,

00:23:44.410 --> 00:23:47.270
I've been far more focused on
flexibility, soft tissue work,

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:49.040
a little muscle gain,
which has been amazing.

00:23:49.040 --> 00:23:53.150
But I gained enough weight
where just, you know, I don't

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:53.983
care about the weight.
- You feel it.

00:23:53.983 --> 00:23:55.540
- I just want to feel nice in my clothes.

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:57.042
I just want to feel great.

00:23:57.042 --> 00:24:00.276
So I was like okay, if I could
just get seven to 11 off,

00:24:00.276 --> 00:24:02.371
real fast, and the thing
that works for me is fasting.

00:24:02.371 --> 00:24:05.560
But for me, I would fast for a full day,

00:24:05.560 --> 00:24:08.177
which usually meant I wouldn't
eat for about 36 hours.

00:24:08.177 --> 00:24:11.182
- [Rabiah] Yeah, 'cause
you include the sleep.

00:24:11.182 --> 00:24:12.015
- But for three days a week?

00:24:12.015 --> 00:24:13.050
Explain it to me real quick.

00:24:13.050 --> 00:24:17.810
- So I do Sundays, Tuesdays, Thursdays.

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:18.870
- So Sunday you don't eat?

00:24:18.870 --> 00:24:20.568
- So Saturday I stop.
- Night, you eat.

00:24:20.568 --> 00:24:22.900
- 10 p.m. on Saturday.

00:24:22.900 --> 00:24:24.300
- You eat for real.

00:24:24.300 --> 00:24:25.590
- So Saturday I would eat for real.

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:28.040
I would stop eating at 10.
- By 10, okay.

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:28.941
- And Monday, so today,
I fasted yesterday.

00:24:28.941 --> 00:24:33.500
Today, I would break my fast at 10 a.m.

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:34.333
- [Gary] Okay.

00:24:34.333 --> 00:24:35.730
- So you have the sleep on both nights

00:24:35.730 --> 00:24:37.496
As part of this.
- Yeah, yeah, interesting.

00:24:37.496 --> 00:24:39.980
- And the reason why I did it, I was doing

00:24:39.980 --> 00:24:41.790
the intermittent fasting
that everyone else

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:44.510
is doing for a while, which
was like the 16, eight

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:47.040
where you eat during an eight hour window,

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:49.043
so like 11 to whatever.

00:24:51.444 --> 00:24:53.307
- That's like a fake fast.

00:24:53.307 --> 00:24:55.612
I feel like you have to go past 20.

00:24:55.612 --> 00:24:59.320
You have to go a full 24
hours for it to really matter

00:24:59.320 --> 00:25:00.480
in my opinion.
- Well, I agree.

00:25:00.480 --> 00:25:01.313
- Eight hour windows?
- And it's easier.

00:25:01.313 --> 00:25:02.878
- That's just like normal life.

00:25:02.878 --> 00:25:04.630
People eat too much.
- That's exactly right.

00:25:04.630 --> 00:25:07.350
- By the way, when you
were in your career,

00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:09.072
were you a eater during the day or no?

00:25:09.072 --> 00:25:10.270
- During the day?

00:25:10.270 --> 00:25:11.570
- [Gary] Yeah.

00:25:11.570 --> 00:25:13.240
- Yeah, but I could run it off.

00:25:13.240 --> 00:25:14.800
- And you also had food all around you.

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:16.130
It's really funny like--

00:25:16.130 --> 00:25:17.060
- You forget to eat.

00:25:17.060 --> 00:25:18.063
- [Dylan] It's so hard.

00:25:18.063 --> 00:25:22.030
- I have never until I,
actually what's really ironic?

00:25:22.030 --> 00:25:24.340
Four years ago when I
started trying to lose weight

00:25:24.340 --> 00:25:26.919
was the first time in my
life that I ever ate lunch.

00:25:26.919 --> 00:25:27.940
- [Rabiah] That's what I'm saying.

00:25:27.940 --> 00:25:29.947
- My big thing where I
was getting caught was

00:25:29.947 --> 00:25:30.949
no breakfast, no lunch, but I would eat

00:25:30.949 --> 00:25:32.830
like a machine at dinner,

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:34.170
and it would always be like 9 p.m.

00:25:34.170 --> 00:25:36.230
And I would eat like 4,000 calories.

00:25:36.230 --> 00:25:37.410
- [Steve] Yeah.

00:25:37.410 --> 00:25:41.350
- My body, it's so easy for
me to fast if I can get over

00:25:41.350 --> 00:25:43.670
the dinner hump because having breakfast

00:25:43.670 --> 00:25:45.287
and lunch is not natural to me

00:25:45.287 --> 00:25:47.115
'cause of retail.
- Right.

00:25:47.115 --> 00:25:48.696
Yeah, so I think it's the same thing.

00:25:48.696 --> 00:25:50.100
- How long you been doin' it?

00:25:50.100 --> 00:25:53.071
- This is like my third
or fourth week maybe.

00:25:53.071 --> 00:25:53.904
- You feel great right?

00:25:53.904 --> 00:25:56.198
- I feel great, and I
don't mind the fasting.

00:25:56.198 --> 00:25:57.635
- [Gary] I think it's great.

00:25:57.635 --> 00:26:01.484
- I think the other thing is
with traveling like it's hard.

00:26:01.484 --> 00:26:04.950
You can't prep, bah,
bah, bah, so I'm like,

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:07.760
no, this is so much
easier to implement for me

00:26:07.760 --> 00:26:09.924
because I don't want to
be counting my calories.

00:26:09.924 --> 00:26:12.681
I'm still tryin' to
eat well on those days.

00:26:12.681 --> 00:26:13.680
- Who got you on?

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:15.224
A piece of content, a friend?

00:26:15.224 --> 00:26:16.230
- So, yeah, a piece of content.

00:26:16.230 --> 00:26:17.654
I don't know what.
- But something caught you.

00:26:17.654 --> 00:26:22.654
- There's a lady named Sumaya
Kazi who was on The Today Show

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:24.570
or something, and they
interviewed her about this.

00:26:24.570 --> 00:26:25.750
- [Gary] And boom.

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:28.360
- And she had like a
hundred dollar little plan

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:29.820
and I downloaded it

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:32.454
just to have something.
- Good for you.

00:26:32.454 --> 00:26:33.454
I love it.

00:26:33.454 --> 00:26:34.454
- To be accountable with.

00:26:34.454 --> 00:26:35.287
- Sir?

00:26:35.287 --> 00:26:36.458
- Yes?

00:26:36.458 --> 00:26:37.460
- [Gary] What are you
obsessed with right now?

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:38.790
- Well, I'm obsessed with watching

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:40.610
the next generations of our business.

00:26:40.610 --> 00:26:43.420
So when I started, we had eight employees.

00:26:43.420 --> 00:26:45.040
Five of us were family members.

00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:45.873
We had this business.

00:26:45.873 --> 00:26:47.680
Today, we have 19,000 employees.

00:26:47.680 --> 00:26:49.122
- With 18,000 family members?

00:26:49.122 --> 00:26:49.955
(laughs)

00:26:49.955 --> 00:26:52.461
- There's 101 descendants of our parents

00:26:52.461 --> 00:26:53.660
or spouse.
- That's amazing, man.

00:26:53.660 --> 00:26:54.870
- And there's 15 working,

00:26:54.870 --> 00:26:57.023
so now I'm kinda the last
of the first generation

00:26:57.023 --> 00:26:58.350
that were second and third.

00:26:58.350 --> 00:27:01.053
And I'm watching this electric car coming.

00:27:01.053 --> 00:27:03.390
And I'm watching our gas business.

00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:05.940
And I'm looking at the future saying,

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:08.440
this gas business is slowly going away.

00:27:08.440 --> 00:27:11.750
And what are these next
generations gonna do here

00:27:11.750 --> 00:27:13.899
because they're dealing with
it right now on terms of--

00:27:13.899 --> 00:27:16.127
- Do you think they have the
DNA that your dad and you

00:27:16.127 --> 00:27:18.920
and your brother had where you
were willing to put yourself

00:27:18.920 --> 00:27:20.500
out of business in the short term?

00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:21.851
- I think we do.

00:27:21.851 --> 00:27:23.730
You know that's our vision, to put Sheetz,

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:25.010
as we know it today, out of business.

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:26.010
That's our vision.
- Always.

00:27:26.010 --> 00:27:28.030
- But I'm watching the next generation,

00:27:28.030 --> 00:27:31.130
and I'm still there, so
I can feel it with them.

00:27:31.130 --> 00:27:33.240
And I nudge them a little bit, you know.

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:34.270
- And how do they take the nudge?

00:27:34.270 --> 00:27:37.584
Do they take it as Yoda,
as like wise old Yoda,

00:27:37.584 --> 00:27:40.165
or do they take it as like,
get the fuck out of here.

00:27:40.165 --> 00:27:40.998
(laughs)

00:27:40.998 --> 00:27:42.784
You don't know what you're talking about?

00:27:42.784 --> 00:27:43.617
- I think they tell me one thing.

00:27:43.617 --> 00:27:45.286
- [Gary] They tell you Yoda.

00:27:45.286 --> 00:27:46.230
- And they're really thinking,
get the fuck outta here.

00:27:46.230 --> 00:27:47.620
I think that's what they're thinking.

00:27:47.620 --> 00:27:49.203
- Who do you think you
have the most leverage with

00:27:49.203 --> 00:27:51.110
of the family members internally,

00:27:51.110 --> 00:27:52.560
and what's her or his position?

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:54.783
- Well, I don't know leverage with but--

00:27:54.783 --> 00:27:57.996
- When I say leverage, I mean
there is that wisdom, respect,

00:27:57.996 --> 00:28:00.119
and you can see she or he taking it.

00:28:00.119 --> 00:28:03.210
- I'm starting to feel it
with the second and third

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:04.800
generation, and there are some

00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:06.750
that are so damn sharp, it's unbelievable.

00:28:06.750 --> 00:28:09.510
I knew they were sharp, but
do they have that feeling

00:28:09.510 --> 00:28:11.616
that says, you know,
that competitive spirit

00:28:11.616 --> 00:28:13.320
that says we gotta make it.

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:14.477
- They have a level of entitlement

00:28:14.477 --> 00:28:16.380
that you and I didn't grow up with.

00:28:16.380 --> 00:28:17.547
- [Steve] Absolutely.

00:28:17.547 --> 00:28:18.380
- And that's not their fault.

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:19.550
It's their reality.

00:28:19.550 --> 00:28:20.549
- But some of it's timing, too.

00:28:20.549 --> 00:28:21.600
Some of them think they want to run away

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:23.480
from this gas business, you know.

00:28:23.480 --> 00:28:25.930
And I'm like you can't run away from it.

00:28:25.930 --> 00:28:29.182
When we sign this lease for
25 years, or whatever it is,

00:28:29.182 --> 00:28:32.537
we're signing it based on
this gas volume and profits.

00:28:32.537 --> 00:28:34.310
It's not going away tomorrow.

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:35.970
And then they say to me,
one of them said to me,

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:37.640
You know you've got your head in the sand.

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:39.820
I said, no, I don't have
my head in the sand.

00:28:39.820 --> 00:28:42.708
I'm just trying to make sure
we get the timing right here

00:28:42.708 --> 00:28:46.130
of transitioning without
gas at some point.

00:28:46.130 --> 00:28:48.180
- You just said something
really, really important

00:28:48.180 --> 00:28:50.090
that I'm gonna harp on
for everybody listening

00:28:50.090 --> 00:28:52.090
who a lot of you are
trying to figure me out

00:28:52.090 --> 00:28:54.740
and I keep saying, watch
what I do, not what I say.

00:28:54.740 --> 00:28:57.000
And more importantly,
I do break this down,

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:59.110
but for some reason, people don't hear it.

00:28:59.110 --> 00:29:03.140
I always talk about, a lot of
people want to give me credit

00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:04.670
for predicting the future,
and I'm always like,

00:29:04.670 --> 00:29:05.503
I'm not Nostradamus.

00:29:05.503 --> 00:29:06.953
I don't predict.

00:29:06.953 --> 00:29:07.786
I'm just good at what's already happened.

00:29:07.786 --> 00:29:08.990
You're talking about
a very important thing

00:29:08.990 --> 00:29:10.180
that you and I clearly share.

00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:12.464
I had a funny feeling
just on the narrative

00:29:12.464 --> 00:29:14.385
that we would have some
similarities, but as I'm listening,

00:29:14.385 --> 00:29:16.424
it's maybe even further than I thought.

00:29:16.424 --> 00:29:21.045
I'm humongous believer in not
overly respecting the past

00:29:21.045 --> 00:29:25.170
but not being to ideological
about the future.

00:29:25.170 --> 00:29:29.190
And I almost see everybody
caught in one of the two.

00:29:29.190 --> 00:29:30.950
And I'm listening to you,
and I'm like, oh shit.

00:29:30.950 --> 00:29:31.783
He's not.

00:29:31.783 --> 00:29:35.180
He's more like me, which is,
he's not head in the sand,

00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:37.350
loved what got him here.

00:29:37.350 --> 00:29:38.930
I've just been listening to your words.

00:29:38.930 --> 00:29:42.880
But in my business everyone's
like, Gary, VR, AR,

00:29:42.880 --> 00:29:45.580
even voice that I'm very
passionate about with Alexa.

00:29:45.580 --> 00:29:48.030
If you noticed, watch
what I do, not what I say,

00:29:48.030 --> 00:29:51.010
I established it's coming.

00:29:51.010 --> 00:29:53.430
But I've been unbelievably
quiet for the last six months,

00:29:53.430 --> 00:29:55.490
mainly because the progress is slow,

00:29:55.490 --> 00:29:57.630
and I'm not excited about it.

00:29:57.630 --> 00:29:58.730
I couldn't agree more.

00:29:58.730 --> 00:29:59.690
You have people come in.

00:29:59.690 --> 00:30:02.080
And that's not what you're saying,

00:30:02.080 --> 00:30:03.120
but for everybody listening,

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:05.160
I'm watching a lot of
people come into businesses,

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:07.260
whether kids or whether executives

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:08.770
and they want to make their mark.

00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:10.580
And the way they think
they can make their mark

00:30:10.580 --> 00:30:14.130
is by betting on the future
and then looking right.

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:17.322
The problem is, sometimes it
takes longer than you think.

00:30:17.322 --> 00:30:19.530
The problem is, sometimes you

00:30:19.530 --> 00:30:21.450
give up something that's valuable.

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:22.940
Yes, do I think electronic cars?

00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:24.710
Sure, I also think self-driving cars

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:26.920
are gonna be a very important variable

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:28.900
because then you can make B2B deals,

00:30:28.900 --> 00:30:30.530
not with individuals, if there's only

00:30:30.530 --> 00:30:32.150
four companies that control all the cars.

00:30:32.150 --> 00:30:35.130
They don't need the middle
man of the gas station.

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:39.240
It could be macro deals with
Exxon or Shell or whoever.

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:42.270
Nonetheless, we all know
those things are coming,

00:30:42.270 --> 00:30:45.130
but I think what your experience
and my experience, look.

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:49.453
In 1998, when I took over
operations for my dad's business,

00:30:49.453 --> 00:30:52.870
two years after I launched
Wine.Library.com, ecommerce,

00:30:52.870 --> 00:30:57.410
I thought by the year 2000,
everybody's gonna be buying wine

00:30:57.410 --> 00:30:59.490
from me on the internet,
and I've gotta go crazy.

00:30:59.490 --> 00:31:00.890
Now, it worked out for me because

00:31:00.890 --> 00:31:02.600
I was right about the macro.

00:31:02.600 --> 00:31:06.980
So betting the farm with
frantic pace on the internet

00:31:06.980 --> 00:31:11.980
was good, but if I was a
VC or had a big company,

00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:14.400
not a small company
that didn't have money,

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:15.740
I would have definitely over-invested.

00:31:15.740 --> 00:31:17.230
We just didn't have any money.

00:31:17.230 --> 00:31:18.683
So I just invested what?

00:31:18.683 --> 00:31:19.683
- [Rabiah] Yeah.

00:31:19.683 --> 00:31:20.863
- You know, we didn't have a credit line.

00:31:20.863 --> 00:31:21.865
We weren't funded.

00:31:21.865 --> 00:31:23.132
We weren't a big company.

00:31:23.132 --> 00:31:26.090
So if I was running
VaynerMedia as a 22-year-old,

00:31:26.090 --> 00:31:27.742
I would have wasted a lot of money.

00:31:27.742 --> 00:31:28.900
I just didn't have.

00:31:28.900 --> 00:31:31.292
The luxury of not having is very powerful.

00:31:31.292 --> 00:31:33.890
I think you are absolutely right.

00:31:33.890 --> 00:31:36.880
Like plenty of cars are
going to go into gas stations

00:31:36.880 --> 00:31:39.140
in four years, lots of them.

00:31:39.140 --> 00:31:40.060
- [Steve] Absolutely.

00:31:40.060 --> 00:31:41.439
- There's nothing that's happened yet

00:31:41.439 --> 00:31:43.850
that would make me think
that's not the case.

00:31:43.850 --> 00:31:46.410
- Yeah, if I did, I wouldn't be signing

00:31:46.410 --> 00:31:49.026
20, 25 year leases because it's dependent.

00:31:49.026 --> 00:31:50.270
- Yeah look, 20, 25
years, who knows right?

00:31:50.270 --> 00:31:52.488
Like that's a ways, but to your point.

00:31:52.488 --> 00:31:53.321
- [Steve] I agree.

00:31:53.321 --> 00:31:55.098
- Start ripping out gas stations.

00:31:55.098 --> 00:31:55.931
- [Steve] Yes.

00:31:55.931 --> 00:31:57.101
- Would be insanity.

00:31:57.101 --> 00:31:58.140
- [Steve] Yep, yep.

00:31:58.140 --> 00:32:00.098
- I might, with you, if
we were like two people,

00:32:00.098 --> 00:32:02.900
I'd be like, hey, do we want to completely

00:32:02.900 --> 00:32:06.550
build a model 25 years
from today on gas stations?

00:32:06.550 --> 00:32:07.770
That's an interesting debate.

00:32:07.770 --> 00:32:08.603
- [Steve] Correct.

00:32:08.603 --> 00:32:10.679
- Ripping out gas stations
would be insanity.

00:32:10.679 --> 00:32:13.960
- And I think that you have to
look back at history as well.

00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:16.670
The first 20 years we
spent without gasoline.

00:32:16.670 --> 00:32:18.280
We didn't have gasoline.

00:32:18.280 --> 00:32:21.171
Then all of a sudden, you build
this dependency on gasoline.

00:32:21.171 --> 00:32:22.172
- [Gary] Well, of course.

00:32:22.172 --> 00:32:23.460
- But you know, going forward we don't

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:24.293
have to be a gas station.

00:32:24.293 --> 00:32:26.220
We have to look at our core competencies.

00:32:26.220 --> 00:32:27.810
We can operate in six states.

00:32:27.810 --> 00:32:31.080
We know how, obviously,
to create a culture

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:32.910
and an atmosphere where
people want to thrive.

00:32:32.910 --> 00:32:35.170
You have to look at the core competencies

00:32:35.170 --> 00:32:36.360
and say what else can we do?

00:32:36.360 --> 00:32:37.740
You know we can run restaurants.

00:32:37.740 --> 00:32:38.573
We can run car washes.

00:32:38.573 --> 00:32:40.410
We can run one of the core competencies.

00:32:40.410 --> 00:32:42.590
Think outside of the
convenience store business.

00:32:42.590 --> 00:32:43.450
What else can we run?

00:32:43.450 --> 00:32:45.680
- Or in the same way that
the biggest transition

00:32:45.680 --> 00:32:47.410
the company ever really made was,

00:32:47.410 --> 00:32:49.410
you know, you didn't go into
full detail, but there was

00:32:49.410 --> 00:32:53.050
a transition at some point of
supplying dairy in one form

00:32:53.050 --> 00:32:55.950
and then deciding to
be the retailer of it.

00:32:55.950 --> 00:32:56.783
Right?

00:32:56.783 --> 00:32:58.028
- [Steve] Exactly.

00:32:58.028 --> 00:33:00.400
- Look, I mean, I think I
would argue that your company

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:03.850
has permission to be the transportation

00:33:03.850 --> 00:33:05.740
infrastructure in some shape or form.

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:07.820
Your company absolutely could
have been the people that

00:33:07.820 --> 00:33:11.943
invented the electronic scooters
that we see in California.

00:33:11.943 --> 00:33:12.944
- [Steve] Sure.

00:33:12.944 --> 00:33:13.945
- You could.

00:33:13.945 --> 00:33:14.948
- [Steve] Sure.

00:33:14.948 --> 00:33:15.949
- You could.

00:33:15.949 --> 00:33:16.782
I mean, you absolutely could.

00:33:16.782 --> 00:33:19.879
And I think Nintendo started
as a playing card company.

00:33:19.879 --> 00:33:23.170
The biggest movies in Hollywood
are made from something

00:33:23.170 --> 00:33:25.310
that started off as a comic book company.

00:33:25.310 --> 00:33:26.390
Superhero movies, right?

00:33:26.390 --> 00:33:29.584
So I do think when you take,
to your point, the energy,

00:33:29.584 --> 00:33:33.213
it's about finding new containers
and merchandising them.

00:33:33.213 --> 00:33:34.046
- Right.

00:33:34.046 --> 00:33:36.560
And you know we go outside.

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:39.630
We spent three days in Seattle last month.

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:40.790
We got to visit Amazon.

00:33:40.790 --> 00:33:42.605
We got to visit one of the

00:33:42.605 --> 00:33:43.438
fulfillment centers.
- You got to see the stores

00:33:43.438 --> 00:33:44.606
that don't have any cash.

00:33:44.606 --> 00:33:45.606
Yeah I get it.

00:33:45.606 --> 00:33:46.607
It's smart.

00:33:46.607 --> 00:33:47.610
- We went into the store.

00:33:47.610 --> 00:33:48.443
They're going to open
3,000 of 'em in the next

00:33:48.443 --> 00:33:49.613
three years.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:33:49.613 --> 00:33:50.840
- And who wants to go into a store

00:33:50.840 --> 00:33:52.995
where you have to waste
four minutes for a cashier

00:33:52.995 --> 00:33:54.740
when you can just grab and go.

00:33:54.740 --> 00:33:55.573
- [Steve] Exactly.

00:33:55.573 --> 00:33:56.420
- A hundred thousand percent.
- And we got to meet

00:33:57.502 --> 00:33:59.740
with the innovation officer at Starbucks

00:33:59.740 --> 00:34:01.810
who has 60 people working with her.

00:34:01.810 --> 00:34:03.247
And so we're out there just kinda looking

00:34:03.247 --> 00:34:06.142
and Carnegie Mellon we spent
three days with last month.

00:34:06.142 --> 00:34:07.368
We're just constantly out there looking,

00:34:07.368 --> 00:34:10.849
but I see that in the
next generation, too.

00:34:10.849 --> 00:34:11.850
- [Gary] Good for you.

00:34:11.850 --> 00:34:12.683
- Which I'm happy with you know.

00:34:12.683 --> 00:34:14.094
- And so where are you
spending your physical time?

00:34:14.094 --> 00:34:16.730
- Well, I've got the Sheetz Fellows,

00:34:16.730 --> 00:34:19.810
so I teach 12 times a semester.

00:34:19.810 --> 00:34:21.791
I mentor some of the students.

00:34:21.791 --> 00:34:24.450
I got the Family Council.

00:34:24.450 --> 00:34:25.620
We just met the other day.

00:34:25.620 --> 00:34:29.190
You know, we study businesses
that failed as well as

00:34:29.190 --> 00:34:31.649
those family businesses that survived.

00:34:31.649 --> 00:34:33.964
And one of the biggest
reasons they fail is

00:34:33.964 --> 00:34:36.903
that the family ceases to get along.

00:34:36.903 --> 00:34:37.736
- [Gary] That's right.

00:34:37.736 --> 00:34:39.740
- So we've got 101 family members.

00:34:39.740 --> 00:34:40.629
15 work.

00:34:40.629 --> 00:34:44.164
As you go in time, the bigger
percentage doesn't work

00:34:44.164 --> 00:34:46.920
but owns stock, and they want to sell out.

00:34:46.920 --> 00:34:48.260
I want cash.

00:34:48.260 --> 00:34:51.880
And then you got this inner
group saying, that works there,

00:34:51.880 --> 00:34:53.290
I want to hold on.

00:34:53.290 --> 00:34:54.750
And that's the constant battle.

00:34:54.750 --> 00:34:58.680
So as a Sheetz Family
Council and the head of it,

00:34:58.680 --> 00:35:00.530
I'm trying to keep the family together.

00:35:00.530 --> 00:35:01.580
So we do a retreat.

00:35:01.580 --> 00:35:03.080
We get together Thanksgiving.

00:35:03.080 --> 00:35:04.350
We get together Christmas.

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:07.210
And we're together all the
time, and I'm trying to hold

00:35:07.210 --> 00:35:09.210
us together because for that family,

00:35:09.210 --> 00:35:12.630
we want to be a privately
held family business.

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:14.020
That's why we work.
- Let me ask you a question.

00:35:14.020 --> 00:35:14.858
- [Steve] Yep.

00:35:14.858 --> 00:35:18.232
- You went through this
important transition

00:35:18.232 --> 00:35:21.530
of five years, right, from going from

00:35:21.530 --> 00:35:22.950
an operator to a leader right?

00:35:22.950 --> 00:35:24.200
- [Steve] That's correct.

00:35:25.610 --> 00:35:26.810
- I have to assume.

00:35:26.810 --> 00:35:29.851
I don't know anything, and I already know

00:35:29.851 --> 00:35:34.851
that you're a disproportionate
glue to this ambition.

00:35:35.080 --> 00:35:38.420
I assume, you, at this point,
are giving a lot of thought

00:35:38.420 --> 00:35:42.613
to who becomes the glue when
you decide not to be the glue.

00:35:42.613 --> 00:35:45.200
Is that where your spending
a lot of time thinking, too?

00:35:45.200 --> 00:35:46.397
- Well, some of it.

00:35:46.397 --> 00:35:49.210
- And can multiple people be the glue?

00:35:49.210 --> 00:35:50.920
'Cause I've been thinking
a lot about that, too.

00:35:50.920 --> 00:35:54.650
- Well, you know, when my
brother was CEO, we had a rule.

00:35:54.650 --> 00:35:57.160
We actually established that one person,

00:35:57.160 --> 00:36:00.800
one family member, would be
the last one to say, yes.

00:36:00.800 --> 00:36:02.498
One decision maker.
- [Gary] No two cooks

00:36:02.498 --> 00:36:03.498
in the kitchen.
- He passed it to me.

00:36:03.498 --> 00:36:05.287
- I believe in that.

00:36:05.287 --> 00:36:06.570
- Then he passed it to my nephew.

00:36:06.570 --> 00:36:09.330
But the next one came along and said,

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:11.470
I'm not sure I'm comfortable with that.

00:36:11.470 --> 00:36:13.346
So now we have three.

00:36:13.346 --> 00:36:14.179
It's me.

00:36:14.179 --> 00:36:15.670
It's my two nephews.

00:36:15.670 --> 00:36:16.698
There's three of us.

00:36:16.698 --> 00:36:17.531
- [Gary] Interesting.

00:36:17.531 --> 00:36:19.297
- So when I faze out, I faze out.

00:36:19.297 --> 00:36:23.600
In two years, off of
kinda the Family Council,

00:36:23.600 --> 00:36:24.790
the other two have to agree.

00:36:24.790 --> 00:36:26.280
We have to agree on who this

00:36:26.280 --> 00:36:27.980
third person is.
- Third.

00:36:27.980 --> 00:36:28.813
Interesting.

00:36:28.813 --> 00:36:31.740
- Yeah, so it was one, one,
one and the next one said,

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:35.058
I'm not comfortable with one
making all these decisions.

00:36:35.058 --> 00:36:36.401
- [Gary] I understand. I understand.

00:36:36.401 --> 00:36:37.403
- So there's three.

00:36:37.403 --> 00:36:38.830
So now we'll decide which one's the next.

00:36:38.830 --> 00:36:40.084
- What else is goin' on in your world?

00:36:40.084 --> 00:36:43.220
What else do you want
entrepreneurs, executives,

00:36:43.220 --> 00:36:45.923
other people to know, think,
or what questions do you have,

00:36:45.923 --> 00:36:47.583
or what do we want to jam on?

00:36:47.583 --> 00:36:52.142
So I'm focused, the
scaling part, on really,

00:36:52.142 --> 00:36:56.320
we have been pivoting from
like, a peer consulting company

00:36:56.320 --> 00:36:58.750
to where we supported a bunch

00:36:58.750 --> 00:37:01.160
of technology companies privately.

00:37:01.160 --> 00:37:03.110
We're trying to get into
the government business

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:07.450
as well as the Federal
government providing IT services.

00:37:07.450 --> 00:37:11.010
And so now we're building
out this IT Plus model,

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:15.306
which we haven't finalized
yet, but basically building on

00:37:15.306 --> 00:37:17.250
what you guys were just talking about.

00:37:17.250 --> 00:37:20.500
This whole digital
transformation that every company

00:37:20.500 --> 00:37:25.500
has to be really thinking about.

00:37:26.020 --> 00:37:27.630
How do you do business, and how are you

00:37:27.630 --> 00:37:30.359
using technology to
get that business done?

00:37:30.359 --> 00:37:33.800
- Oh, my god, I always tell
people it's like electricity.

00:37:33.800 --> 00:37:34.633
- [Rabiah] And they're failing.

00:37:34.633 --> 00:37:37.360
- When electricity first came
around, there was businesses.

00:37:37.360 --> 00:37:38.490
- [Rabiah] Yep.

